    the help of Corollary~@cor:engel-niltriangular-basis of Engel's theorem. The
    “only if” part follows from Problem~@pr:exp-semisimple-nilpotent-criterion.]

  #hint[@pr:prove-unipotent-group-exp-isomorphism][The first part of the theorem
    follows from Problem~@pr:unipotent-group-iff-unipotent-algebra and
    corollaries of Engel's theorem. The surjectivity of $exp: frak(g) -> G$
    follows from the fact that together with $A$ the group $G$ contains the
    subgroup $G(A) = {exp t X: t in CC}$, where $X = log(A)$, cf.
    Theorem~@th:unipotent-closure-additive-group. The remaining properties of
    the map $exp$ are proved with the help of
    Problem~@pr:exp-log-nilpotent-unipotent.]

  #hint[@pr:differential-semisimple-nilpotent][Notice that the element $d f(xi)$
    is contained in the tangent algebra of the algebraic subgroup
    $f(G(xi)) subset H$.]

  #hint[@pr:representation-semisimple-nilpotent][Make use of the hint to
    Problem~@pr:differential-semisimple-nilpotent and of
    Problem~@pr:exp-semisimple-nilpotent-criterion.]

  #hint[@pr:prove-jordan-decomposition-tangent][Let $G(xi) = T times U$, where
    $T$ is a torus and $U$ a vector group. If
    $frak(g)(xi) = frak(t) plus.o frak(u)$ #source(145)is the corresponding
    decomposition of the tangent algebra then the decomposition
    $xi = xi_s + xi_n$, where $xi_s in frak(t)$ and $xi_n in frak(u)$, is the
    desired one. The uniqueness of the desired decomposition follows, due to
    Problem~@pr:representation-semisimple-nilpotent, from the uniqueness of the
    additive Jordan decomposition of a linear operator.]

  #hint[@pr:locally-faithful-reflects-semisimple][Consider the Jordan
    decomposition of $xi$ and make use of
    Problem~@pr:representation-semisimple-nilpotent.]

  #hint[@pr:semisimple-nilpotent-preimage][Take any pre-image and consider its
    Jordan decomposition.]

  #hint[@pr:complexification-subgroup-criterion][
    Problem~@pr:group-real-form-from-involution implies that a connected
    algebraic subgroup $H subset G(CC)$ is a complexification of an algebraic
    subgroup of $G$ if and only if $tau(H) = H$ and this, in turn, is equivalent
    to the fact that $d tau(frak(h)) = frak(h)$. Next, make use of
    Problem~@pr:real-subspace-conjugation-invariant.]

  #hint[@pr:real-algebraic-subalgebra-criterion][Notice that if the subalgebra
    $frak(h)(CC)$ is algebraic then the corresponding connected algebraic
    subgroup of $G(CC)$ is according to
    Problem~@pr:complexification-subgroup-criterion a complexification of an
    algebraic subgroup of $G$. The second statement follows from the first one.]

  #hint[@pr:torus-with-commuting-semisimple][By
    Corollary~@cor:semisimple-element-in-torus of
    Theorem~@th:borel-subgroups-cover-group $g$ is contained in a torus. Let
    $H subset G$ be the subgroup generated by this torus and $S$. This is a
    connected algebraic subgroup by Theorem~@th:generated-subgroup-algebraic and
    $g$ belongs to its center. Let $T$ be a maximal torus of $H$ containing $S$.
    By Corollary~@cor:central-semisimple-in-maximal-tori of
    Theorem~@th:borel-subgroups-cover-group $T in.rev g$.]

  #hint[@pr:prove-torus-centralizer-connected][It suffices to prove that any
    semisimple element of $Z(S)$ is contained in $Z(S)^0$ but this follows from
    Problem~@pr:torus-with-commuting-semisimple.]
]
